-- title:
--   Nilpotence of coordinates descends along an isogeny
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a Noetherian commutative ring and let $\varphi,\psi\colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to B[\![X_0,X_1]\!]$ be pairs of power series in two variables over $B$ (the type `Series B`), and let $d$ be a natural number. Assume that each component of $\varphi$ and each component of $\psi$ has zero constant coefficient, and that $\varphi$ satisfies `HasKernelOfDegree φ d`, i.e. the quotient algebra $B[\![X_0,X_1]\!]/(\varphi_0,\varphi_1)$ is finite as a $B$-module and projective as a $B$-module, and for every field $\kappa$ and every ring homomorphism $f\colon B \to \kappa$ the $\kappa$-vector space $\kappa[\![X_0,X_1]\!]/(f(\varphi_0),f(\varphi_1))$, the coefficients of $\varphi$ being pushed forward along $f$, has dimension exactly $d$. Assume further that there is an $N$ such that for both $i$ the power $X_i^N$ lies in the ideal generated by the two series $(\psi\circ\varphi)_i = \psi_i(\varphi_0,\varphi_1)$, the substitution of $\varphi$ into $\psi_i$. The conclusion is that there exists an $N'$ (in general different from $N$) such that for both $i$ the power $X_i^{N'}$ lies in the ideal generated by $\psi_0$ and $\psi_1$.
--
--   This is the descent step used to cancel an isogeny from a composite kernel: nilpotence of the coordinates modulo $(\psi\circ\varphi)$ forces nilpotence modulo $(\psi)$ once $\varphi$ has a finite projective kernel algebra of constant degree, the mechanism being injectivity of substitution by $\varphi$ on $B[\![X_0,X_1]\!]$. It feeds the results on kernels of composites of homomorphisms of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules, in particular [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.HasKernelOfDegree.of_comp`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.HasKernelOfDegree.of_comp) and [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.HasKernelOfDegree.le_and_of_comp_pow`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.HasKernelOfDegree.le_and_of_comp_pow).
-- source:
